  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Public Service Map",
    "overview": "Shepherd looks like a civic service, not a product launch. The map is the subject; everything around it is plain white paper with navy and blue ink, organised like a good government information page: a solid navy masthead, a thin tinted status strip, then content in calm, bordered panels. Nothing competes with the hazard, which is the only warm colour on screen.\n\nThe two surfaces share one system at two densities. The dashboard (emergency managers, desktop) is a three-column command layout: controls, map, stats and feeds. It is dense but quiet, with small uppercase section labels and tabular numbers. The phone (residents, ~390px) is the same vocabulary scaled for thumbs and stress: larger targets, softer 10px corners on the controls you press, one primary action per view, and an always-visible action bar.\n\nThe visual rejections are explicit and binding (see Do's and Don'ts): no gradients, no glass, no glows, no emoji icons, no default \"AI website\" fonts, no decorative charts. Depth comes from 1px borders and tint, never from shadows.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "White surfaces, navy chrome, blue as the single interactive and route colour.",
      "Orange means hazard and red means closed road. Neither colour ever appears for any other purpose.",
      "Flat. 1px borders and the tint fill separate surfaces.",
      "Public Sans throughout, with tabular numerals for every time, ETA, distance and stat.",
      "Touch targets of at least 44px everywhere and at least 48px for primary phone actions."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Two Warnings Rule",
        "body": "Orange is the hazard; red is a closed road. Neither may be used for decoration, emphasis, branding, or generic errors.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The One Ink Rule",
        "body": "Blue is the only interactive colour. Anything blue is either clickable, selected, or a route/prediction on the map.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Quiet Basemap Rule",
        "body": "The OpenStreetMap raster is desaturated (saturation −0.85, contrast −0.15, opacity 0.6) over Map Paper, so Shepherd's own layers are always the loudest thing on the map.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
